A||||16||watts||'88 Rear brakes||||1||
Z||000000||watts||11-03-2001||11:24 PM||||Ok, I'm not dead sure where I read this, but I decided to give it a whirl and see what happens.<P>Someone had mentioned the front brake rotors off of a '93 cavalier would possibly fit the rear of an '88 Fiero.  More or less they do.<P>Seems that the only problem is the outboard pad.  You either have to mill them down, or have them custom made.  Now, this will vary from one manufacturer to the next (minor changes in the rotor offset, thickness, etc), but in MY experiment I had to cut the outboard pad down to 10.75mm's measured from the back of the backing plate to the pad surface.<P>Needless to say - if you choose to do this, you do so at your own risk, your mileage may vary, etc etc.<P>I make no claim that this was MY idea (and whoever found it out first, feel free to jump in here!).  I'm just saying that it worked for me.<BR><P>------------------<BR>--------------------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||11-04-2001||12:24 AM||||Did you mill the back of the pad (the metal surface) or the asbestos part?  Just curious..what was your pad width before?  I just want an idea of how much shaving is involved, and if it would be worth the money savings for me to do it.  I don't anticipate getting pads before I upgrade my brakes, but it's always nice to know what your options are.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>Edited for confusing grammar.<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||watts||11-04-2001||01:29 AM||||Sorry - I ground off the friction material.<P>It started at about 14mm, had to take it to about 10.5 - 11mm so the rotor would turn.<P>As for cost savings...  I priced the rotors out in town here at about $140cdn each.  The Cav ones were about $50.  I'd say that's a whack of savings!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||11-04-2001||03:48 AM||||Did you just remove material from one pad, or both?  I'm wondering...if we get the cavalier rotors, what combination of maching could we use to get them in there.  I would think you could turn down the rotor 1mm on each side (2mm total) and maybe give the thicker pad of the two (usually one's a little more worn than the other due to single piston action) a scuffing treatment with a grinder, you could get it.  You'd have to get a millimeter or two out of the pad, but that's easy enough to do.<P>The only reason I'm curious if it'd be just as achievable with turning the rotor down is because in my experience, the cheaper rotors need to be turned before being used anyways.  I doubt the 88 rear brake pads are cheap (haven't priced them out, don't know) but I'm guessing the cavalier rotor is cheaper than the pads, thus we should try to avoid clearancing the expensive part to put in the cheap part.  What do you think, looking back, would this have been doable?  Thanks for the info.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||watts||11-04-2001||06:11 PM||||I only had to mill the outboard pad.<P>The inner one's happy, as you can retract the piston in far enough to fit (with plenty of room).<P>I'll have to pull my bills out, but I'm pretty sure the pads were actually cheaper than the rotors.  I got fairly high quality rotors, so maybe that's why they were more.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||11-04-2001||06:50 PM||||I had the Cavalier rotors all the way around on 88 and did not have to do anything to the rear. Now the front. . . that is a different story!||||reg||1
Z||000006||watts||11-04-2001||07:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>I had the Cavalier rotors all the way around on 88 and did not have to do anything to the rear. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Like I was saying..  really depends on the brand of pad you use, since they have different starting points in thickness.  What brand were they?  I'll pick up a pair and compare them to what I've got here.  Be a sight easier than going through what I did!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||11-04-2001||07:59 PM||||That has been too long ago- what ever brand AutoZone sells.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||11-04-2001||08:43 PM||||I realized while driving today that I don't HAVE to install new brake pads at the same time as the rotors.  I have slightly warped rotors, just noticeable at certain speeds.  Since my pads are already "broken in" I could just bolt in the new rotors without any modification....right?  I don't think I'll be doing this anytime soon, unless the rotors start showing more warp, but it's always nice to know I can.  Also, it's my understanding that the front and rear rotors are all the same, from what I've read elsewhere.  Is this true?  Thanks for the info, I love to learn the "little things" that can make a big difference when you need it to.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
